  "live_records": {
    "q-38011": {
      "dom_id": "step-q38011",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{4},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{4} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}+4{x}_{4} \\\\ 7{x}_{4}-4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -1 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b]$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -2 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -1 & -3 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 10 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -1 & 17 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 10 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -16 & 32 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has three pivot columns (the $1$st, $2$nd and the $4$th). Hence ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the third equation we get \n\n$$\n-16{x}_{4}=32\\,⟹\\,{x}_{4}=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}+10(-2)=-10\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=5+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting ${x}_{2}=5+{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}=2$ into the first equation we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(5+{x}_{3})-2(-2)=5\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-4-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4-{x}_{3} \\\\ 5+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=0$, we have\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere $T(x)=b.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{4}=-4+5-2=-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
