    "q-38618": {
      "dom_id": "step-q38618",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $w+x-z,$ where $w,x,y,z$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}w \\\\ x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}w+3z-x-2y \\\\ 4x-2w+y-6z \\\\ 3w+5x+10z-16y \\\\ 5y-w-3z-x\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -16 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b]$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -16 & 10 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 5 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -10 & 1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 & -20 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the fourth equation, we get the false statement $0=12.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that  $b\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
